-- title:
--   Frobenius acts as Uₚ on toric ℓ^k-torsion
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a level $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the whole kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$, and assume $j$ lies in the level-one $q$-expansion function field over $\mathbb{Q}$. The data are: a model $\mathfrak{X}$ of type `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`; an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of the geometric function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` which, on Laurent series, sends any element agreeing with an element $u$ of the level-$M/p$ field `xHFunctionFieldBar (M/p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)` to `qExpand _ p` applied to $u$; the hypothesis `hwgen` that on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathfrak{X}$.`Meta.C` the correspondence $\mathfrak{X}$.`w` induces, through `pointEquivPlace`, the action of the semilinear automorphism attached to $\theta$; a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit, residue field algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, together with a ring map $\rho : R\,p \to A$ compatible with the structure map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; level data $\Lambda$ over $A$ whose $\Lambda$.`f` satisfies the abelian-scheme property bundle (smooth, proper, connected fibres, a relative group law), with $\Lambda$.`σA` the map induced by $\rho$; a Néron object $O$ over these data whose designation $(O.G, O.g, \text{unit})$ represents the relatively algebraically trivial sub-Picard functor (`hD`); the Hecke and diamond inputs `hin` for level $M$; a set of primes $S$ and a prime $\ell$. Two further hypotheses describe the singular-point combinatorics: `hTOR` says that for every permutation of $O$.`ssFinset` acting on both coordinates by the mod-$\ell$ $q$-expansion Frobenius place, every Frobenius element at $p$ in the decomposition group of $A$, and every point of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ at level $M$ whose section reduces to the node-unit class of a family $w$, the Galois translate reduces to the node-unit class of $t \mapsto p \cdot w(\mathrm{perm}^{-1} t)$; `hUPtor` says that $U_p$ acts on node-unit classes by precomposition with a permutation $\sigma_N$ of $O$.`ssFinset` satisfying $(\sigma_N n)_2 = n_1$. Given finally a Frobenius element $\varphi$ at $p$ lying in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, the conclusion is that for every $k$ and every $xt$ in the subgroup $O$.`toricPts` $(\ell^k)$ (generated by the toric points of order dividing $\ell^k$), the translate $\varphi \cdot xt$ again lies in $O$.`toricPts` $(\ell^k)$, and $U_p(\varphi \cdot xt)$, i.e. `genOpH` applied to the generator `CohCarrier.Gen.U p _ hpM`, equals the multiple of $xt$ by the natural number underlying the image in $\mathbb{Z}/\ell^k$ of the value of the $\ell$-adic cyclotomic character at $\varphi$.
--
--   This is the local statement at $p$ in the analysis of the toric part of the reduction of the Jacobian at a prime exactly dividing the level: on $\ell^k$-torsion of the toric subgroup, the Hecke operator $U_p$ composed with Frobenius acts by the cyclotomic character, the form in which the $\ell$-adic monodromy contribution enters Ribet's level-lowering argument. It is used by [`ModularCurve.toricFrobeniusHecke_toricMonodromyPart_jZero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.toricFrobeniusHecke_toricMonodromyPart_jZero).
